{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>If seed be sown</b> — seed of the kinds of legumes. זֵרוּעַ is a noun, as in \"and they give us of the seeds\" (Daniel 1:12). <b>It is pure</b> — to teach you that it has not been prepared and rendered susceptible to become food that can receive impurity until water comes upon it. This is the language of Rashi. Now, above, Scripture mentioned (Leviticus 11:34) \"of all food that may be eaten, upon which water comes\" to teach about this preparatory susceptibility, but there the Sages said that food does not contract impurity through the air space of an earthenware vessel except with preparation through water. And here it adds that even from the creeping things themselves it does not receive impurity except with such preparation; thus it is explicit in Torat Kohanim (chapter 11, paragraph 5). And it is also possible to say that \"of all food that may be eaten\" mentioned above refers to meat and cooked foods that were mentioned there, and here it says that even seeds, when they bring them out for sowing, contract impurity through preparation with water. And the reason for the preparation is that the filth of the creeping thing and of the impure things adheres to foods through moisture, but not so with dry things. And the Torah made an extra safeguard to render impure those that were prepared with water and then dried, so that we not make our words into precise measurements. And behold, the law of preparatory susceptibility also applies to the impurity of carcasses, and Scripture mentioned it here for the matter we have spoken of."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The citation 'Torat Kohanim (chapter 11, paragraph 5)' resolves פרק י\"א ה as the 11th chapter's 5th paragraph, following the standard division of Sifra on this parashah."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:06.389714608Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}